Title: Stripboard Baseline
Author: DraftLine tests

EXT. HARBOR - DAWN

Mist over the water. Gulls argue on the pilings.

MARIE
We go now, before the tide turns.

THEO
(not looking up)
The tide turned an hour ago.

INT. FERRY - PASSENGER DECK - DAY

Benches bolted to the floor. A radio plays kompa, badly tuned.

MARIE
Sit. Eat something.

THEO
I'm not hungry.

MARIE
You're always hungry.

INT. FERRY - PASSENGER DECK - CONTINUOUS

Theo pushes past a crate of chickens to the rail.

THEO
How long?

AUGUSTIN (O.S.)
Four hours, if God is kind.

EXT. HIGHWAY - MARIE'S CAR (MOVING) - NIGHT

Headlights cut the rain. Marie drives with both hands.

MARIE (V.O.)
I promised him I'd bring the boy home.

INT. CHILDHOOD HOME - KITCHEN - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Young Marie, ten, kneads dough beside her mother.

GRANDMÈRE
Slower. Bread is patient.

INT./EXT. FERRY - WHEELHOUSE - NIGHT

Augustin steers by memory. The compass light flickers.

AUGUSTIN
Hold on to something.

EXT./INT. PIER - MOMENTS LATER

The ferry knocks against the pier. Ropes thrown, caught, tied.

I/E VAN - DAY

Theo in the back of a tap-tap, squeezed between strangers.

EST. PORT-AU-PRINCE - NIGHT

The city's lights climb the hills.

.FERRY BAR - NIGHT

A forced heading with no INT. or EXT.

THEO
Two colas.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE

No time of day in this heading at all.

EXT. CROIX-DES-BOSSALES - NIGHT (DREAM)

Market stalls empty and lit by candles. Theo walks alone.

INT. FERRY - CABIN - LATER

Marie sleeps sitting up. Theo watches her.

THEO
You said you'd tell me everything.

MARIE
I said I'd tell you when we got there.

THEO
We're almost there.

EXT. BEACH - SUNSET

The ferry, small on the horizon. Theo writes his name in the sand, then lets the water take it. The light goes orange, then pink, then a colour there is no word for. He sits and waits for it to go.

Wave 1. The tide comes in, goes out, comes in again. Theo counts them under his breath, the way his father taught him, and loses count, and starts again from one.

Wave 2. The tide comes in, goes out, comes in again. Theo counts them under his breath, the way his father taught him, and loses count, and starts again from one.

